  "statement": "The Skeptic's sharpest point deserves to be said plainly: an executive order is a memo, not a law, and this one names no switch-holder, no trigger, and no penalty. That critique lands because concentrated shutdown power has historically fallen hardest on the politically weak, and because developers often end up writing their own compliance rules. The real disagreement is not whether that diagnosis is right. It's what follows from it. The Skeptic sees a vacuum and concludes the fix is better-specified human authority. The evidence points somewhere else: the fix is removing human discretion from the trigger entirely.\n\nHere is the distinction that matters for everyday life. A hospital running an AI triage system, a county processing disability claims with automated review — these systems will run whether or not California is ready. The question is what happens when something goes wrong at 2 a.m. A discretionary switch means one official decides, on judgment, with no rule telling them what counts as proof. A triggered switch means the system's own measurements trip a pre-set threshold — the way a circuit breaker works, or the way the Food and Drug Administration's recall authority works, bound to evidence rather than to whoever holds office that year. The order as written specifies neither holder nor trigger, which means it defaults to the worst version: a stop-power with no written standard. Nobody should accept that, including people who want AI deployed fast.\n\nAnd there's a cost nobody in this debate has counted yet: a wrong halt is itself a harm. If an AI system is genuinely load-bearing — screening mammograms, adjudicating claims — switching it off is not a neutral safety act. It pushes the work back onto backlogged humans, and the people waiting in that backlog pay the price. The order has no procedure for weighing halt-induced harm against the risk a halt stops, and no procedure for counting the benefits a pause quietly erases. An executive office with no statutory criteria has never had to count foregone benefits, and this memo doesn't ask it to start. That asymmetry — visible harms counted, invisible benefits ignored — will bias any discretionary switch toward stopping, regardless of whether stopping is the safer choice.\n\nSo here is the trade worth making. Grant the halt authority — binding, real, backed by the state — but only in exchange for two things priced as the cost of that power, not as favors to industry. First, mandatory incident reporting and third-party access to runtime data, the same deal aviation got with its near-miss reporting system: you fly fast, you report everything. Second, triggers written in advance, tied to measured failures, with the thresholds published. This is honest about its own limits: no threshold regime has been tested on autonomous AI yet, and diffuse, slow-moving harms — a child-facing app that quietly misleads — may never trip a clean threshold. For those, a narrow discretionary backstop stays, but it carries a burden of proof before it fires. Watch for one thing as this order becomes regulation: whether the trigger gets written down, or whether the whole design quietly becomes one person's judgment call with a stopwatch on it.",
