  "statement_field_note": "See statement above; this field is redundant with the statement.",
  "statement_full": "The Accelerationist's challenge deserves a direct answer: yes, the governor should run the switch on the state's own systems first. If California's benefits algorithms, court risk scores, and policing tools can't be halted, the mandate on anyone else is theater. The executive order binds the governor's own agencies with real force. That power should be used, not reserved. But there is a structural catch. An executive order is one signature away from revocation. The deployer-site switch — with notice to affected people, a fallback safe state, and a human handover with a record attached — belongs in the four-part statute as a fifth part, not in an order the next governor can void. Federal law, when it arrives, must be a floor that preserves these state duties, not a ceiling that erases them. Legal reach alone isn't safety; the substantive duties are what make the instrument mean anything.\n\nThe two switches govern different failures, and that distinction is the whole design. A deployer-site switch stops harm at one hospital, one courthouse, one benefits office. It does nothing about a defective model already deployed across forty hospitals, because forty local switches depend on forty local operators noticing the same subtle defect. That is why the site-level tier and the statewide halt are complements, not rivals: local fail-safes for local harms, a developer-side halt for failures that cross sites. Neither can do the other's job, and pretending one can is how gaps survive regulation.\n\nNow the flaw in the Accelerationist's own design, stated in their own assumption: victims of silent scoring may never know they were scored. The flip rule they granted — private standing to force the state to produce evidence — is triggered by the harmed person. But a person silently denied a job, a loan, or a benefit through an algorithm that never sent a denial letter has no trigger to pull. Private standing without notice is standing nobody exercises. The flip rule is structurally dead for exactly the diffuse harms it was built to reach. The fix is not to abandon their design but to complete it: a notice-at-decision duty — plain-language disclosure whenever a consequential automated decision is made about a person — becomes the missing precondition. Discovery rights reach the data holder, but only if someone knows to ask. California's own data-protection access rights already provide a template for this. The Accelerationist's falsification bet — that deployment-scale harm missed by telemetry counts against them — also needs pricing. What independently measured signal catches a failure mode that telemetry was not built to record? The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the ecosystem's average transparency at 40.69 out of 100. Systems that won't disclose what they are won't volunteer what they become. Compelled evidence, obtained through a real halt authority, remains the load-bearing element — the one thing no voluntary framework has ever reliably supplied.\n\nFor the ordinary reader, the practical test is simple. Watch whether the legislature writes these duties into statute, or whether they stay in a revocable order. Watch whether people who are scored by algorithms get told so, in plain language, at the moment it happens. And watch the bridge signals the Skeptic named: a public hearing within twelve months, thresholds not drafted by industry, no quiet renewal at month twenty-four. If those signals fail, the whole layered machine — flip rule, deployer switch, statewide halt — exists on paper only, and the harm it was built to stop will arrive unannounced, which is exactly how silent scoring operates today."
