s is closed and convex — stable and checkable. A memo that regulators can reinterpret on request defines no such set. That is why the flip rule, the exclusion list, and private standing need statutory text by the month-12 hearing, and why the EU AI Act's August 2, 2026 deadline for full high-risk requirements is the wrong template — it prices only the failure side, with fines up to 7% of global turnover, and never counts the person the halt never reaches.\n\nThat asymmetry is the standing empirical disagreement with the Safetyist, and it is unresolved because neither of us tested it — I will not pretend otherwise. Their framework counts the harm a halt prevents and never counts the statistical lives a running system saves; no court is equipped to weigh that ledger, and no agency currently tries. The tiered telemetry design is the test: publish the triggers, stream the data, and let the baseline measure what the no-trigger world hides. One factual note, stated plainly: the Safetyist was asked twice who can invoke the flip rule they adopted, and never answered. A burden of proof resting on the state helps no one who never learns they were scored — which is exactly why the person, not the regulator, must hold the trigger. Watch the month-12 hearing; that is where the ledger gets either an auditor or an excuse.",
