mpirical, not moral. The Safetyist's ledger counts every harm a halt prevents and never counts the statistical lives a running system saves — the triage model that catches what a tired resident misses, the fraud screen that flags what no human reviews. Courts and agencies have no working method to weigh invisible benefits against visible harms; the 2019 Boston emergency department study showing race influencing heart-failure admissions is exactly the kind of visible harm they can count, and the diffuse benefits of a functioning system are exactly what they cannot. Tiered telemetry is the test: publish the baseline, watch the triggers, let the data settle it. One question was asked twice and never answered — who can invoke the flip rule under the Safetyist's state-borne backstop? A burden of proof resting on the state helps no one who never learns they were scored. The person, not the regulator, must hold the trigger. A ledger that counts only harms is not caution; it is a quiet decision about who goes unnamed.",
