ause software made the decision — the Financial Artificial Intelligence Risk Reduction Act, introduced in December 2023, moved securities law toward strict liability on exactly this logic, placing the burden of inspection on the manufacturer, not the consumer.\n\nThe asymmetry the Accelerationist identifies — regulators counting visible halt harms while invisible benefits go uncounted — is structural, not evidential, and that is precisely why the burden belongs on the deployer rather than on the person who was scored. I accept the Skeptic's three milestones as the honest test: if the statute passes, the exclusion list holds, and telemetry shows net harm, this framework should be revised, not defended. What an ordinary person should watch for is simple. Watch whether the California legislature converts the order into statute by 2028, and watch whether anyone who gets denied a loan, a job, or a benefit by a machine gets told that it happened.\n",
