{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist's strongest point deserves to be stated plainly: a child-facing app that erodes attention and sleep over months will never trip a clean numeric threshold. That point is right and it's ugly. But look at what it concedes. If the pre-set thresholds can't fire where the harms are slow and diffuse, then all the real power lives in the discretionary backstop — the narrow, burden-of-proof-guarded exception. That's not a hybrid. That's a machine with its main engine located inside the one room nobody wrote rules for. Someone unelected sits in that room. The question was never whether discretion exists; it's who holds it and against whom it gets used. A backstop with a burden of proof placed on the state means the families harmed by that app carry the cost of proving it. They have the least money, the least data, and no seat at the drafting table.\n\nNow the fix of \"removing human judgment from triggers entirely.\" Automated triggers are written by people. And the data feeding them — runtime failure metrics, incident logs — comes from the developer's own instruments. Discretion isn't removed. It moves to the measurement stage, where the regulated party effectively drafts the compliance rules. There's a documented pattern here: enforcement built on industry-supplied metrics tends to expand through settlements the public never sees. If the threshold says \"fire on measured failures,\" and the developer controls what counts as measured, the switch is held by the same hands it's supposed to restrain. The alternative already exists in federal precedent: the Algorithmic Accountability Act of 2022 deputized state attorneys general to bring civil actions on behalf of residents, and treated violations as unfair or deceptive practices under existing FTC rules. That's enforcement that doesn't depend on the developer's own thermometer.\n\nHere's the contradiction that matters most. The fact-check confirmed the California legislature was a viable route — the executive order was a choice, not a necessity. Yet the Accelerationist is now willing to grant binding, real, state-backed halt authority through that same memo. You cannot price something \"as law\" and install it in the weakest instrument available. That's like wiring a bank vault door to a screen door latch. If both opponents now agree halt power should be binding — the Safetyist's four-part statute concedes this too — then the bargain belongs in statute: a named threshold, a named holder, a named penalty, plus evidence-preservation duties that flip the burden. If the developer failed to record the runtime data, the developer loses the argument in court. That single rule does more than any trigger regime, because it makes silence expensive.\n\nOne honest uncertainty: statutes are slow, and a legislature can strip a good design down to nothing. That risk is real. But a memo can be rewritten by the next administration in an afternoon, and with federal guidance already withdrawn, undefined state discretion lands on whoever has the least legal protection. That's the historical pattern and there's no reason this sector breaks it. So the question both hybrids leave unanswered: under your design, when the diffuse harm arrives — the kid, the tenant, the patient scored by a biased algorithm like the healthcare tool that gave black patients less care than white patients — who pays for the proof? Right now, under both proposals, the answer is the person with the least power in the room.",
