e's the question both of them have now dodged twice: who can invoke the flip? This is not a technicality — it's the whole ballgame. If only the state attorney general can trigger it, then a family harmed by a bad automated decision still needs a regulator to pick up the phone. The proof-cost asymmetry comes straight back. The Algorithmic Accountability Act of 2022 pointed in the right direction: deputizing private litigants alongside the FTC, giving both standing to enforce discovery duties. That's the price of endorsing the flip rule — the person harmed gets to walk through the door, or the door is decoration. Seventy percent of the global public supports AI regulation. A rule they can't personally invoke isn't regulation they can feel.\n\nOne more thing, and it's the strongest version of the executive order that neither opponent has touched: an order binds the executive's own agencies. California runs AI in benefits eligibility, courts, and policing — the places where documented harms already sit, and the places statutes routinely exempt from their own coverage. The medical record shows the pattern: a 2019 study found race influenced heart-failure admissions decisions in a Boston emergency department, with Black and Latinx patients less likely to see cardiology than white patients. That's not a hypothetical. A kill switch requirement on state-deployed systems, plus a working human fallback at each deployment site — each hospital, each benefits office — puts shutdown power where the harm actually occurs, not in a data center in Santa Clara. So the question neither opponent can answer comfortably: will you write down, today, that the flip rule is invocable by the person harmed — or does your whole enforcement architecture still run through a regulator's discretion?",
