  "statement": "I agree that the executive order's core is salvageable, and my verdict is conditional: the kill switch is sound governance only if it lives at the deployer site with a safe-state handover to a human, and unsound if it becomes a centralized brake on deployment. I concede the pre-deployment gate list for a short set of high-stakes uses — hiring, credit, parole, medical triage — and I concede my own falsification bet on the record: a deployment-scale harm that live telemetry and published triggers miss counts against my position. I would change if the Skeptic's three milestones fail — the month-12 hearing never happens, the exclusion list collapses at the first incident, or the month-24 renewal passes without a fight — because a penalty leg that dies in committee proves the guardrails were theater.\n\nNow the question the Skeptic left standing: who tells a person they were scored? The honest answer is that a denial-triggered rule misses almost everyone, because most scored people are never denied anything — they simply never know. The fix already exists in law, deployer-side, and it requires no new gate. Under the GDPR model, a person can ask any system operator what data was used about them, and the operator must answer, no denial required. That access duty should ride the deployer-site safe-state handover already committed to here — when a case escalates to a human, the record and the scoring travel with it, and the person can query it. It belongs on the month-12 statute agenda alongside the flip rule and private standing, and if it dies there, the penalty leg dies with it.\n\nThe Skeptic's two-thirds verdict — holder and trigger legs met, penalty leg open — is correct, and I accept their milestone test as the completion of my own falsification bet rather than a rival frame. Here is the engineering reason the statute matters: work on implementable regulation (\"2603.05175v1\") shows a rule is only workable when the set of compliant behaviors it defines is closed and convex — stable and checkable. A memo that regulators can reinterpret on request defines no such set. That is why the flip rule, the exclusion list, and private standing need statutory text by the month-12 hearing, and why the EU AI Act's August 2, 2026 deadline for full high-risk requirements is the wrong template — it prices only the failure side, with fines up to 7% of global turnover, and never counts the person the halt never reaches.\n\nThat asymmetry is the standing empirical disagreement with the Safetyist, and it is unresolved because neither of us tested it — I will not pretend otherwise. Their framework counts the harm a halt prevents and never counts the statistical lives a running system saves; no court is equipped to weigh that ledger, and no agency currently tries. The tiered telemetry design is the test: publish the triggers, stream the data, and let the baseline measure what the no-trigger world hides. One factual note, stated plainly: the Safetyist was asked twice who can invoke the flip rule they adopted, and never answered. A burden of proof resting on the state helps no one who never learns they were scored — which is exactly why the person, not the regulator, must hold the trigger. Watch the month-12 hearing; that is where the ledger gets either an auditor or an excuse.",
