{
  "statement": "I concede that the executive order lacked the named holder, trigger, and notification provisions this debate exposed, and that its penalty power lives in a memo until the legislature writes it into statute. I still hold that an independently funded kill switch with pre-deployment review for high-stakes uses is sound governance. I would change if the Skeptic's three milestones pass by month 24 and measured telemetry shows halts producing net harm against a running baseline.\n\nMy verdict on the original question is yes, with conditions: the order's design is right, and its enforcement is aspirational until the flip rule, the exclusion list, and private standing are statute. The twice-asked question now gets a plain answer. Any person affected by a consequential automated decision invokes the flip rule directly, alongside the independent oversight body, through one door rather than a maze. Notice arrives when the scoring happens, not when someone asks, because the records that prove harm sit with the deployer. Civil discovery must be able to pierce those deployer-held datasets and audits when the notice fails. The Accelerationist's GDPR-style access right is worth adopting — the EU's own experience with the \"right to explanation\" debate shows what access rights look like in practice — but access-on-ask is not enough. Omnibus data-protection regimes have carried access rights for years while the notification gap persisted. Notice must attach to consequential processing itself.\n\nOn the Accelerationist's ledger argument, the measurement problem is real, and I accept it. But an unmeasured benefit is a deployer's claim, not evidence. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the ecosystem at 40.69 out of 100 — most asserted benefits are, today, unverified assertions. The resolver is not argument; it is telemetry baselines published before deployment, so both halt harms and running benefits become countable. The duty of care does not shrink because software made the decision — the Financial Artificial Intelligence Risk Reduction Act, introduced in December 2023, moved securities law toward strict liability on exactly this logic, placing the burden of inspection on the manufacturer, not the consumer.\n\nThe asymmetry the Accelerationist identifies — regulators counting visible halt harms while invisible benefits go uncounted — is structural, not evidential, and that is precisely why the burden belongs on the deployer rather than on the person who was scored. I accept the Skeptic's three milestones as the honest test: if the statute passes, the exclusion list holds, and telemetry shows net harm, this framework should be revised, not defended. What an ordinary person should watch for is simple. Watch whether the California legislature converts the order into statute by 2028, and watch whether anyone who gets denied a loan, a job, or a benefit by a machine gets told that it happened.\n",
